"[2602.03560v1] HySparse: A Hybrid Sparse Attention Architecture with Oracle Token Selection and KV Cache Sharing (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03560v1)\nciteturn31494view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03560v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 3 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:HySparse: A Hybrid Sparse Attention Architecture with Oracle Token Selection and KV Cache Sharing\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Yizhao Gao , cite6†Jianyu Wei , cite7†Qihao Zhang , cite8†Yu Cheng , cite9†Shimao Chen , cite10†Zhengju Tang , cite11†Zihan Jiang , cite12†Yifan Song , cite13†Hailin Zhang , cite14†Liang Zhao , cite15†Bo Yang , cite16†Gang Wang , cite17†Shijie Cao , cite18†Fuli Luo L12: View a PDF of the paper titled HySparse: A Hybrid Sparse Attention Architecture with Oracle Token Selection and KV Cache Sharing, by Yizhao Gao and 13 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite19†View PDF cite20†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:This work introduces Hybrid Sparse Attention (HySparse), a new architecture that interleaves each full attention layer with several sparse attention layers. While conceptually simple, HySparse strategically derives each sparse layer's token selection and KV caches directly from the preceding full attention layer. This architecture resolves two fundamental limitations of prior sparse attention methods.\nL16: First, conventional approaches typically rely on additional proxies to predict token importance, introducing extra complexity and potentially suboptimal performance. In contrast, HySparse uses the full attention layer as a precise oracle to identify important tokens. Second, existing sparse attention designs often reduce computation without saving KV cache. HySparse enables sparse attention layers to reuse the full attention KV cache, thereby reducing both computation and memory.\nL17: We evaluate HySparse on both 7B dense and 80B MoE models. Across all settings, HySparse consistently outperforms both full attention and hybrid SWA baselines. Notably, in the 80B MoE model with 49 total layers, only 5 layers employ full attention, yet HySparse achieves substantial performance gains while reducing KV cache storage by nearly 10x.\nL18: Comments:  | 17 pages, 2 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite21†arXiv:2602.03560 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite22†arXiv:2602.03560v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite23†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.03560†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Shijie Cao [cite24†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Tue, 3 Feb 2026 14:05:57 UTC (189 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled HySparse: A Hybrid Sparse Attention Architecture with Oracle Token Selection and KV Cache Sharing, by Yizhao Gao and 13 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite19†View PDF L35:   * cite20†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite25†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite26†view license L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite27†< prev |   cite28†next > L44: \nL45: cite29†new | cite30†recent | cite31†2026-02 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite32†cs L50: cite33†cs.AI L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite34†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite35†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite36†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ##